# SRG-APP-000439-WSR-000155  -  CAT II
# Cookies exchanged between the web server and the client, such as session
# cookies, must have cookie properties set to force the encryption of
# cookies.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000439-WSR-000155
group:           V-206438
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      documented
cci:             CCI-002418
control_rev5:    SC-8

contribution:    Splunk can force the Secure attribute on its session cookie in every
                 deployment topology and can set a restrictive SameSite attribute, which
                 are the cookie properties the product exposes for protecting cookies in
                 transit.

governs:         web.conf - Configures the Splunk Web interface.
check:           web.conf [settings] enableSplunkWebSSL in 1|true
check:           web.conf [settings] tools.sessions.secure in 1|true


# --- vendor verification: generated, do not edit by hand
vendor:          web.conf [settings] cookieSameSite https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/web.conf
vendor:          web.conf [settings] tools.sessions.forceSecure https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/web.conf
vendor:          web.conf [settings] tools.sessions.secure https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/web.conf
retired_check:   web.conf [settings] tools.sessions.secure in 1|true
                 reason: "Whether or not the browser must transmit session cookies over an
                         HTTPS connection when Splunk Web is configured to serve requests
                         using HTTPS (the 'enableSplunkWebSSL' setting is 'true'.) If set to
                         'true' and 'enableSplunkWebSSL' is also 'true', then the browser
                         must transmit the session cookie over HTTPS connections. This
                         improves session security. Default: true"
retired_check:   web.conf [settings] cookieSameSite in lax|strict
                 reason: 10.4.2 spec: "cookieSameSite = [ not_specified | lax | strict |
                         none ]... A value of 'lax' means the Splunk web server sets the
                         'SameSite=Lax' attribute. A value of 'strict' means...
                         'SameSite=Strict'. A value of 'none' means... to let browsers send
                         the cookies in all contexts... The 'SameSite=None' attribute in a
                         cookie requires that you set the 'Secure' attribute... Default:
                         lax". The 10.0.0 spec states "Default: not_specified".
# --- end vendor verification

external:        Browser enforcement of the Secure and SameSite attributes
external:        Any reverse proxy that rewrites or re-issues cookies before they reach the
                 client
external:        Any embedding requirement for dashboards in a third-party iframe, which
                 web.conf.spec:676-682 states requires cookieSameSite = none and is an
                 organizational decision
external:        The secure bit on a cookie returned through a TLS-terminating proxy, which
                 web.conf [settings] tools.sessions.forceSecure governs only where
                 tools.proxy.on is true; with no proxy configured the setting is inert and
                 a test on it reports a finding against an instance that serves TLS
                 directly.

citation:        SRG-APP-000439-WSR-000155 check text; web.conf.spec:664-682, 951-969
finding:         Splunk does not force protected cookie properties: tools.sessions.secure
                 or tools.sessions.forceSecure is false, or cookieSameSite is none or
                 not_specified, so a cookie can be sent outside an encrypted, same-site
                 context.
